const ECONOMICS_EDEXCEL_B_WRITTEN = {
  '1.1': {
    name: 'Scarcity, Enterprise & How Markets Work',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define price elasticity of demand.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Price elasticity of demand measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price. It is calculated as the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price.' },
      { q: "State two factors of production other than enterprise, and the reward earned by each.", marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: "Two from: (1) Land - all natural resources, rewarded by rent. (2) Labour - the physical and mental effort of workers, rewarded by wages. (3) Capital - man-made aids to production such as machinery and premises, rewarded by interest. One mark for each correctly named factor with its correct reward." },
      { q: "Define added value.", marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: "Added value is the difference between the selling price of a good or service and the cost of the bought-in materials and components used to make it. For example, a bag sold for £65 using £18 of leather and fittings has added value of £47. Added value is not profit: it must still cover wages, rent and other overheads." },
      { q: 'State the conditions necessary for market equilibrium.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Market equilibrium occurs when: (1) Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at the prevailing price. (2) There is no tendency for the price to change. (3) The market clears with no excess demand or excess supply.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: "Explain two business objectives, other than profit maximisation, that a firm might pursue.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Two from the specification, each explained (2 marks each). (1) Sales maximisation: the firm sells the largest volume that still covers its costs, cutting price and promoting heavily. It does this to build market share, which can lower unit costs as fixed costs are spread more widely and can strengthen its buying power over suppliers. (2) Survival: for a new firm, or any firm in a downturn, keeping cash coming in matters more than the margin on each sale, so price may be set below the profit-maximising level. Other creditable objectives: market share, cost efficiency, return on investment, employee welfare, customer satisfaction and social objectives. A strong answer notes that where salaried managers rather than owners take the decisions, growth and security are often rewarded more than profit - the divorce of ownership from control." },
      { q: 'Explain why the demand for insulin is likely to be price inelastic.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Insulin is price inelastic because: (1) It is a necessity for diabetics with no close substitutes - patients must take it regardless of price. (2) It forms a relatively small proportion of total income for most users. (3) There is no time to find alternatives as it is needed immediately and regularly. These factors mean quantity demanded changes very little when price changes.' },
      { q: 'Explain, using a diagram, how a price ceiling set below equilibrium affects a housing market.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'A price ceiling (maximum price) set below equilibrium means the market cannot reach its natural equilibrium. At the lower controlled price: quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, creating a shortage. Landlords may reduce supply as lower rents reduce profitability, while more tenants demand housing at the lower price. This leads to non-price rationing mechanisms such as waiting lists, favouritism, or a decline in property quality as landlords cut maintenance spending.' },
      { q: "Explain what is meant by creative destruction and why it matters for an economy.", marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Creative destruction is the process by which entrepreneurs organise the factors of production into new enterprises and new methods, which displace established firms and ways of working - the same act that creates one business destroys the position of another (1 mark). It matters because the resources released by firms that close, including workers, premises and equipment, are what new entrants use, so churn is the mechanism by which an economy reallocates resources towards higher-value uses (1 mark). ONS Business Demography for 2024 recorded around 317,000 UK business births and around 280,000 deaths, so roughly one business in nine on the register was new in a single year - churn on that scale is a sign of a dynamic economy rather than of failure (1 mark)." },
      { q: "Using a supply and demand diagram, explain how a subsidy on renewable energy affects the equilibrium price and quantity, and who gains from it.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Diagram: a supply and demand diagram with the supply curve shifting vertically downward (rightward) from S to S+subsidy by the amount of the subsidy per unit, showing equilibrium price falling from P1 to P2 and quantity rising from Q1 to Q2 (2 marks for a correctly labelled diagram with both shifts and both new equilibrium values marked). Explanation: the subsidy lowers the effective cost of supplying each unit, so producers are willing to supply more at every price. Consumers gain because they pay a lower price and buy more; producers gain because the price they receive, including the subsidy, is above the old equilibrium price (1 mark). Evaluation: how the gain is split between the two depends on how price-sensitive demand is - with inelastic demand most of the subsidy is retained by producers - and the whole scheme is funded by taxpayers, so the gain to this market is a cost elsewhere (1 mark)." },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain how the imposition of a specific indirect tax affects the equilibrium price and quantity in the market for cigarettes.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'A specific indirect tax of t per unit shifts the supply curve vertically upward by the amount of the tax from S to S+t. The new equilibrium price rises from P to P1 and the equilibrium quantity falls from Q to Q1. Consumers pay a higher price per pack while producers receive an effective price of P1 minus t, lower than the original price. Because demand for cigarettes is highly price inelastic (addiction, no close substitutes), the rise in price is large relative to the fall in quantity, so the bulk of the tax burden is borne by consumers. The government collects revenue equal to t multiplied by Q1, and a small deadweight welfare loss arises from the lost transactions.' },
      { q: 'Explain how income elasticity of demand (YED) can be used by a firm selling luxury cars to forecast changes in demand during an economic boom.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'YED measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in income, calculated as the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in income. Luxury cars have a YED greater than 1, so during a boom a 5% rise in real incomes might lead to a 10-15% rise in demand. The firm can plan capacity expansion, build inventory ahead of the upturn, and increase marketing budgets in anticipation. Conversely, the firm should also prepare for a sharper-than-average fall in demand during a downturn, since YED works in both directions, and may want to diversify into less income-elastic segments to smooth revenue over the cycle.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, illustrate how a price floor set above the equilibrium price in the market for milk would affect producer revenue and consumer welfare.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a supply and demand diagram with original equilibrium at P, Q, then set a minimum price Pf above P. At Pf, quantity supplied (Qs) exceeds quantity demanded (Qd), creating a persistent surplus. Producers receive a higher price per unit, but the impact on total revenue depends on PED: with inelastic demand, revenue rises; with elastic demand, the fall in quantity outweighs the higher price and revenue falls. Consumers pay more and buy less, and some mutually beneficial transactions between Qd and the original equilibrium quantity simply do not happen. The government may also incur fiscal costs if it buys up the surplus to support farmers.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain why the burden of an indirect tax falls more heavily on consumers when demand is price inelastic than when demand is price elastic.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw two markets with the same upward-sloping supply curve and identical specific tax t. In the inelastic market the demand curve is steep, so when supply shifts up to S+t, the equilibrium price rises sharply (from P to P1) while quantity falls only slightly. The consumer burden is the rectangle (P1 - P) x Q1 and is large, while the producer burden (P - P2) x Q1 is small (where P2 is the price retained by producers after tax). In the elastic market the demand curve is flat, so price rises only a little and most of the burden falls on producers. This is why governments tax goods with inelastic demand like tobacco and fuel - it maximises revenue and minimises political opposition from consumers.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that indirect taxes are always the best method to reduce consumption of goods with negative externalities.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for: Indirect taxes internalise external costs, moving output towards the socially optimal level. They use the price mechanism, maintaining consumer choice. They generate revenue that can fund related public services. They are relatively simple to implement. Arguments against: Difficulty measuring the exact externality means the tax may be set too high or too low. Demand for demerit goods (alcohol, tobacco) tends to be inelastic, so large taxes are needed for small consumption reductions, making them regressive. Alternative approaches (regulation, information provision, nudges) may be more effective for specific goods. Government failure is possible through lobbying or unintended consequences. Evaluation: Effectiveness depends on the specific market, elasticity of demand, and whether alternative policies could achieve the same outcome with fewer distortions. A combination of approaches often works best.' },
      { q: 'Evaluate the extent to which the concept of elasticity is useful for government policy-making.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Usefulness: PED helps predict tax revenue and the incidence of taxation. YED helps forecast demand changes during economic cycles. it helps predict how quickly supply responds to price incentives. the closeness of substitutes helps assess the competitive impact of policy. Limitations: Elasticity values are estimates based on past data and may not hold in future. Ceteris paribus assumptions rarely hold in practice. Values change over time as markets evolve. Measurement difficulties exist for new products or markets. Evaluation: Elasticity provides a useful framework for policy analysis but should be used alongside other evidence, with recognition of its limitations and the dynamic nature of markets.' }
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Credit, Market Failure, Costs & Profit',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define the term "market failure".', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Market failure occurs when the free market mechanism fails to allocate resources efficiently, resulting in a misallocation of resources where the socially optimal level of output is not achieved. This means marginal social cost does not equal marginal social benefit.' },
      { q: "State two sources of finance available to a firm that do not involve borrowing.", marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: "Two from: retained profit reinvested in the business; the owner's own capital or personal savings; the sale of assets the firm no longer needs; issuing share capital to new investors; venture capital; funds raised from individual investors or through online collaborative funding (crowdfunding). One mark each. Leasing is also creditable as a way of using an asset without buying it, though it does create an ongoing payment obligation." },
      { q: 'Define the term "negative externality".', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'A negative externality is a cost imposed on a third party not directly involved in the production or consumption of a good, for which no compensation is paid. It causes social costs to exceed private costs.' },
      { q: 'State two examples of information failure in markets.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two examples: (1) Consumers underestimating the health risks of smoking due to addiction and optimism bias. (2) Asymmetric information in the used car market where sellers know more about vehicle quality than buyers (the "lemons problem").' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: "A firm sells a product for £50. Variable cost is £30 a unit and fixed costs are £120,000 a month. Calculate the contribution per unit and the break-even output, showing your working.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Contribution per unit = selling price - variable cost per unit = £50 - £30 = £20 (2 marks, 1 for the formula and 1 for the answer). Break-even output = total fixed costs divided by contribution per unit = £120,000 / £20 = 6,000 units a month (2 marks, 1 for the formula and 1 for the answer). Check: at 6,000 units revenue = 6,000 x £50 = £300,000 and total cost = £120,000 + (6,000 x £30) = £120,000 + £180,000 = £300,000, so the two are equal and the answer is correct. Allow full marks for a correct answer reached by any valid method, provided the working is shown." },
      { q: 'Explain why merit goods tend to be under-consumed in a free market.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Merit goods are under-consumed because: (1) Information failure - consumers undervalue the long-term benefits (e.g., education\'s impact on lifetime earnings). (2) Positive externalities exist that individuals do not consider - vaccination benefits the wider community through herd immunity. (3) Short-termism and present bias mean consumers discount future benefits. The marginal social benefit exceeds marginal private benefit, so free market consumption falls below the socially optimal level.' },
      { q: "Explain the difference between profit and cash flow, and why a profitable firm can still be forced to close.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Profit is revenue minus costs over a period and is recorded when a sale is made; cash flow is money actually entering and leaving the bank account and is recorded when it moves (2 marks for a clear distinction). They differ because of credit terms, stock and the timing of large one-off payments. A firm selling on 60-day terms while paying its own suppliers in 30 days has to fund that gap every month out of its own resources, so the profit sits in the debtors ledger rather than the bank (1 mark). Growth makes this worse rather than better, because more sales require more stock and more unpaid invoices before any of the extra cash arrives - overtrading. If the firm cannot pay wages, suppliers or tax when they fall due, it can be forced to close while still reporting a profit (1 mark)." },
      { q: 'Explain two reasons why government intervention to correct market failure might itself lead to government failure.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Reason 1: Imperfect information - the government may not know the exact size of an externality, leading to taxes or subsidies set at the wrong level. For example, setting a carbon tax too low fails to correct the externality while setting it too high causes deadweight loss. Reason 2: Unintended consequences - a policy may create distortions elsewhere. For example, a minimum alcohol price may reduce consumption but creates a black market, increases cross-border shopping, and disproportionately affects low-income moderate drinkers rather than the target group of problem drinkers.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain how a negative externality of production leads to market failure and a deadweight welfare loss.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'In a market with a negative externality of production, the marginal social cost (MSC) exceeds the marginal private cost (MPC) by the external cost. On a diagram, the MSC curve lies above the MPC (supply) curve. The free market produces at Q1 where MPC = MPB (demand), but the socially optimal output is Q* where MSC = MSB. The overproduction (Q1 - Q*) creates a deadweight welfare loss shown by the triangle between MSC and MSB from Q* to Q1. This area represents the net cost to society of units produced where the social cost exceeds the social benefit. Government intervention such as a Pigouvian tax equal to the external cost per unit can shift MPC up to MSC, reducing output to the socially optimal level.' },
      { q: "A firm has fixed costs of £120,000 a month, a selling price of £50 and variable cost of £30 a unit. Its forecast output is 8,000 units. Calculate the margin of safety and explain what it tells the firm.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Contribution per unit = £50 - £30 = £20. Break-even output = £120,000 / £20 = 6,000 units. Margin of safety = forecast output - break-even output = 8,000 - 6,000 = 2,000 units (2 marks for the correct figure with working). As a percentage of forecast output that is 2,000 / 8,000 x 100 = 25.0% (1 mark). Interpretation: sales can fall by 2,000 units, a quarter of the forecast, before the firm makes a loss, so the margin of safety measures how much bad news the firm can absorb. It is also worth 2,000 x £20 = £40,000 of monthly profit. Check: revenue = 8,000 x £50 = £400,000, total cost = £120,000 + (8,000 x £30) = £360,000, profit = £40,000, which agrees (1 mark)." },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain how the under-consumption of a merit good such as education results in market failure and how a subsidy could correct it.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a market diagram with the MPB (private demand) curve lying below the MSB curve by the size of the positive externality from education (e.g. higher productivity for employers, lower welfare claims). The free market equilibrium is where MPB = MPC at quantity Qm, which is below the socially optimal Q* where MSB = MSC. The deadweight welfare loss is the triangle between MSB and MSC from Qm to Q*, representing units that society values more than they cost but that are not produced. A subsidy equal to the external benefit per unit shifts the MPC curve downward (or effectively raises private benefit), moving equilibrium consumption to Q* and eliminating the welfare loss. The cost is the subsidy multiplied by Q*, financed by taxpayers.' },
      { q: 'Explain how a minimum unit price on alcohol could reduce harmful consumption, and identify two limitations of this policy.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'A minimum unit price sets a legal floor above the market price for the cheapest, strongest alcohol - typically the units consumed most heavily by problem drinkers and young drinkers. By raising the price of these units, the policy reduces quantity demanded, especially since demand for cheap alcohol tends to be more elastic than for premium brands. Limitation 1: addiction means overall demand for alcohol is relatively inelastic, so the reduction in heavy drinking may be smaller than hoped. Limitation 2: the policy is regressive - low-income moderate drinkers pay more without receiving the benefit, while wealthier heavy drinkers are largely unaffected. Cross-border purchasing and the diversion of revenue to retailers rather than government are further weaknesses.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, illustrate the impact of a Pigouvian tax on a market where production generates a negative externality.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a market with the MPC curve lying below the MSC curve by the size of the external cost (e.g. pollution from a factory). The free market equilibrium is at Qm where MPC = MPB, which exceeds the socially optimal Q* where MSC = MSB. A Pigouvian tax set equal to the marginal external cost shifts the supply curve up from MPC to MSC, raising price from Pm to P* and reducing output to Q*. The deadweight welfare loss from overproduction (triangle between MSC and MSB from Q* to Qm) is eliminated and the firm now internalises the external cost. The government collects revenue equal to the tax multiplied by Q*, which can be hypothecated to clean up the externality or compensate affected third parties.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: "Evaluate the view that a growing firm should always prefer a bank loan to issuing new shares when funding an expansion.", marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: "For the loan: the sum is available at once and matches long-term finance to a long-lived asset; interest is a known and predictable cost; and, crucially, ownership and control are not diluted, so all future profits stay with the existing owners. Interest is also a cost against profit. Against the loan: repayments must be met whatever happens to sales, so the firm carries the whole downside risk itself. The bank will want collateral, and will often want a personal guarantee even from a limited company, which partly defeats the point of limited liability. A rise in interest rates on a variable-rate facility raises the cost after the decision has been taken - the Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% at its September 2026 decision, but a firm borrowing for ten years cannot assume that lasts. For shares: no repayment obligation and no interest, so the risk is shared with the new investors rather than carried by the firm - which matters most when the cash flows from the expansion are uncertain. Venture capital brings expertise and contacts as well as money. Against shares: dilution of ownership and control is permanent, and the new investors take a share of all future profits, not just the profits of this project. Judgement: the view is too strong. The defensible position is to match the source to the risk - debt for the predictable, asset-backed part of an expansion, equity for the genuinely uncertain part, because equity does not have to be repaid if that part fails. The decisive variables are how certain the cash flows are, how geared the firm already is, and whether the owners will accept dilution." },
      { q: "Assess the importance of collateral in determining whether a small firm can obtain credit.", marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: "Why collateral matters: it is an asset the lender can seize and sell if the borrower defaults, so it reduces the loss the bank makes when a loan goes wrong. That lowers the risk premium built into the interest rate, and it also changes the borrower's incentives, because the owner now has their own assets at stake. It is the bank's main answer to asymmetric information - the borrower knows far more about the venture's prospects than the lender does. Why it is decisive for small firms in particular: a young service business often has no property, no heavy equipment and no trading record, so it has nothing to pledge and no history to substitute for it. Assessing a £40,000 loan also costs the bank nearly as much as assessing a £4m one, so small borrowers face proportionally higher costs and more refusals. Counter-arguments: collateral is not the only factor. A strong and verifiable trading record, a credible cash-flow forecast, a personal guarantee, or a government-backed guarantee scheme can all substitute for it. Some finance does not require it at all - trade credit, retained profit, venture capital, crowdfunding and equity from business angels, who price the risk by taking a share of the upside instead. Judgement: collateral is the single most important factor for conventional bank lending to a small firm, because it is what converts an unknown risk into a priced one. But the growth of equity and platform-based finance means it is less of an absolute barrier than it once was - the constraint now is less that credit is unavailable than that the alternatives are more expensive, in interest or in ownership given up." }
    ]
  },
  '2.1': {
    name: 'Productivity & National Economic Performance',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define real GDP.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Real GDP is the total value of goods and services produced within an economy in a given time period, adjusted for inflation. It measures output at constant prices, removing the distorting effect of price changes to show actual changes in the volume of production.' },
      { q: 'State two causes of cost-push inflation.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two causes: (1) Rising energy/commodity prices, such as oil price increases, which raise production costs across the economy. (2) Wage increases that exceed productivity growth, raising unit labour costs for firms which are then passed on as higher prices.' },
      { q: "Define capacity utilisation.", marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: "Capacity utilisation is the proportion of a firm's maximum possible output that it is currently producing, expressed as a percentage: current output divided by maximum possible output, multiplied by 100. A factory able to make 25,000 units a month and currently making 17,500 has capacity utilisation of 17,500 / 25,000 x 100 = 70%." },
      { q: 'State three components of the current account of the balance of payments.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Three components: (1) Trade in goods - exports and imports of physical products. (2) Trade in services - exports and imports of services such as financial services, tourism, and insurance. (3) Primary income - investment income from overseas assets and payments to foreign owners of domestic assets.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain two reasons why GDP per capita may not accurately reflect living standards in a country.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Reason 1: GDP per capita is an average and does not reflect income distribution. A country with high GDP per capita may have extreme inequality where wealth is concentrated among a few while many live in poverty. Reason 2: GDP does not capture non-market activity or quality of life factors. It excludes unpaid domestic work, subsistence farming, and the informal economy. It also ignores environmental degradation, leisure time, and mental wellbeing. A country could have high GDP per capita but poor work-life balance, high pollution, and low social cohesion.' },
      { q: 'Explain why a persistent current account deficit might be a cause for concern for the UK economy.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'A persistent deficit means the UK consistently spends more on imports than it earns from exports. This may indicate declining international competitiveness in manufacturing and goods trade. The deficit must be financed by capital inflows (foreign borrowing or asset sales), making the economy dependent on foreign investor confidence. If confidence falls, capital flight could cause a sharp depreciation and financial instability. Long-term, it may indicate structural weaknesses such as underinvestment in productive capacity, reliance on consumption-driven growth, and deindustrialisation that requires fundamental policy reform rather than short-term fixes.' },
      { q: 'Explain the difference between demand-pull and cost-push inflation, giving an example of each.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Demand-pull inflation occurs when aggregate demand exceeds aggregate supply, pulling prices up. Example: a consumer credit boom increases spending faster than output can grow, creating excess demand. Cost-push inflation occurs when rising production costs push up prices independent of demand. Example: a sharp rise in global oil prices increases transport and energy costs for all firms, leading to higher prices across the economy. The distinction matters for policy: demand-pull responds to interest rate rises, while cost-push may require supply-side solutions.' },
      { q: "Explain how just-in-time stock management lowers a manufacturer's costs, and state one risk it creates.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "How it lowers costs (3 marks): under just-in-time, materials arrive as they are needed rather than being held in a warehouse. (1) Stock sitting on a shelf is cash the firm has already paid out and cannot use, so holding less releases working capital. (2) Storage, handling and insurance costs fall, and floor space can be used for production instead. (3) Less stock means fewer materials become obsolete, damaged or out of date, and quality problems become visible at once because there is no buffer to draw on. The risk (1 mark): with no buffer stock, a single late delivery, strike, breakdown or border delay stops production altogether. The saving is made in every normal year and paid for in the abnormal one, so the value of just-in-time depends on supplier reliability and on how long and complex the supply chain is." },
      { q: 'Using an AD/AS diagram, explain how a rise in oil prices can cause both higher inflation and higher unemployment simultaneously.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'A rise in oil prices increases costs of production across the economy, shifting the short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) curve to the left from SRAS to SRAS1. On the diagram, this moves the equilibrium from point A to point B, where the price level is higher (P1 > P) and real GDP is lower (Y1 < Y). Higher prices represent inflation while lower output means firms produce less and lay off workers, increasing unemployment. This combination of rising prices and falling output is known as stagflation. It poses a policy dilemma: expansionary policy to reduce unemployment would worsen inflation, while contractionary policy to fight inflation would deepen the recession. Supply-side policies to reduce energy dependence are the long-term solution.' },
      { q: 'Using an AD/AS diagram, explain how a fall in net exports caused by a stronger pound affects equilibrium real GDP and the price level.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Start with an AD/AS diagram at equilibrium Y, P. A stronger pound makes UK exports more expensive in foreign currency (reducing X) and imports cheaper in pounds (raising M), so net exports (X - M) fall. This reduces the AD component, shifting AD left from AD to AD1. The new equilibrium has lower real GDP (Y1 < Y) and a lower price level (P1 < P). The negative multiplier amplifies the initial fall as reduced incomes in exporting sectors cause further falls in consumption, raising cyclical unemployment particularly in manufacturing, financial services, and tourism. Inflation also eases, which may give the Bank of England scope to cut rates and partly offset the contractionary effect.' },
      { q: 'Explain two factors that determine the size of a country\'s productivity gap relative to its main competitors.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Factor 1: investment in physical capital. Countries with persistently higher rates of business investment in machinery, automation, and digital infrastructure achieve more output per worker hour. The UK\'s investment-to-GDP ratio is structurally lower than Germany\'s or France\'s, which is a major reason its labour productivity lags by roughly 15-20%. Factor 2: human capital and skills. Countries with stronger vocational training systems, higher tertiary education completion, and continuous in-work upskilling produce more value per worker. The UK\'s relative weakness in mid-level technical skills (apprenticeships, engineering technicians) - compared to the German dual-training system - is frequently cited by the OECD as a structural drag on productivity.' },
      { q: "A factory can produce 40,000 units a month and is currently producing 30,000. Calculate its capacity utilisation and explain two implications of operating at that level.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Calculation: capacity utilisation = current output / maximum possible output x 100 = 30,000 / 40,000 x 100 = 75.0% (2 marks, 1 for the formula and 1 for the answer). Two implications (1 mark each): (1) A quarter of the factory's capacity is idle, and the machinery and premises have to be paid for whether they run or not, so fixed cost per unit is higher than it needs to be - raising output to 36,000 units would lift utilisation to 90% and spread the same fixed costs over 20% more units. (2) The spare capacity is not purely a cost: it means the firm could accept a large new order immediately, absorb a breakdown or an absence, and carry out maintenance without stopping production. Most firms deliberately aim for high but not maximum utilisation for exactly that reason. Accept also: idle capacity signals weak demand to lenders and investors, and can worry the workforce about job security." },
      { q: 'Using an AD/AS diagram, illustrate how a positive supply-side shock (e.g. falling global commodity prices) affects macroeconomic equilibrium.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Start with an AD/AS diagram at initial equilibrium Y, P. Falling commodity prices reduce production costs across the economy, shifting the SRAS curve to the right from SRAS to SRAS1. The new equilibrium is at a higher real GDP (Y1 > Y) and a lower price level (P1 < P), so the economy enjoys higher growth alongside lower inflation. This is a desirable combination - it eases the inflation-unemployment trade-off and gives the central bank scope to support demand if needed. The opposite of stagflation; in the long run it can also shift LRAS right if the cost fall is sustained, raising potential output rather than only short-run output.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate whether GDP growth should remain the primary objective of UK economic policy.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for GDP growth as priority: Growth generates employment, tax revenue for public services, and higher material living standards. It enables poverty reduction and funds investment in infrastructure. It provides fiscal space for addressing other objectives. International competitiveness and geopolitical influence are linked to economic size. Arguments against: GDP ignores environmental degradation and resource depletion. Rising GDP can coexist with increasing inequality. It excludes wellbeing factors like mental health, community cohesion, and leisure. Pursuing growth may conflict with sustainability and climate targets. Alternative measures (HDI, Genuine Progress Indicator, wellbeing indices) may better capture what societies value. Evaluation: GDP growth remains important but should not be the sole objective. A framework incorporating multiple indicators - growth, sustainability, distribution, and wellbeing - better serves long-term societal interests. The weight given to each depends on a country\'s stage of development and democratic choices.' },
      { q: 'Assess whether the UK\'s current account deficit is sustainable in the long run.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments it is sustainable: The deficit is financed by willing foreign investors attracted to UK assets, indicating market confidence. The UK\'s position as a financial centre generates invisible earnings. As long as capital inflows continue, the arithmetic is sustainable. Service exports partially offset goods deficits. Arguments it is not sustainable: The persistent structural nature (post-deindustrialisation) suggests fundamental competitiveness problems. Reliance on foreign capital creates vulnerability to sentiment shifts. Net investment income has turned negative as foreign ownership of UK assets grows. A sudden capital withdrawal could force painful adjustment through sharp depreciation and austerity. Evaluation: Sustainability depends on maintaining foreign investor confidence and whether the deficit reflects productive investment or unsustainable consumption. Structural reforms to boost export capacity and productivity would reduce long-term risks.' }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'Macroeconomic Policy',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define fiscal policy.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Fiscal policy is the use of government spending and taxation to influence aggregate demand and the level of economic activity. Expansionary fiscal policy involves increasing spending or cutting taxes; contractionary policy involves the opposite.' },
      { q: 'State the formula for the multiplier.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'The multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC) or equivalently 1 / MPW, where MPC is the marginal propensity to consume and MPW is the marginal propensity to withdraw (sum of marginal propensity to save, tax, and import).' },
      { q: 'Define quantitative easing.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Quantitative easing is an unconventional monetary policy tool where the central bank creates new money electronically to purchase financial assets (primarily government bonds) from commercial banks and other financial institutions, aiming to increase money supply and stimulate lending and spending.' },
      { q: 'State two supply-side policies that could increase the productive capacity of the economy.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two policies: (1) Investment in education and training to improve human capital, increasing labour productivity and the quality of the workforce. (2) Deregulation of product markets to increase competition, incentivise innovation, and reduce costs for businesses, encouraging enterprise and efficiency.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain how the transmission mechanism of monetary policy works to affect aggregate demand.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'When the Bank of England changes the base rate, this affects: (1) Borrowing costs - higher rates make mortgages and loans more expensive, reducing consumption and investment. (2) Saving incentives - higher rates encourage saving over spending, reducing consumer expenditure. (3) Exchange rate - higher rates attract hot money inflows, appreciating the pound, making exports dearer and imports cheaper, reducing net exports. (4) Asset prices - higher rates reduce house and share prices, creating negative wealth effects that reduce spending. (5) Expectations - rate changes signal the Bank\'s view on inflation, influencing business and consumer confidence. These channels collectively reduce aggregate demand.' },
      { q: 'Explain why the multiplier effect may be smaller in an open economy like the UK.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'In an open economy, a larger proportion of each round of spending leaks out through imports rather than being re-spent domestically. The UK has a high marginal propensity to import (consumer goods, raw materials, components), meaning initial injections quickly flow abroad rather than circulating domestically. Additionally, progressive taxation creates another leakage as higher incomes lead to higher tax payments. These leakages reduce the marginal propensity to consume domestically, producing a smaller multiplier than in a closed economy.' },
      { q: 'Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of using supply-side policies rather than demand-side policies to reduce unemployment.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Advantage: Supply-side policies (e.g., retraining, deregulation) address the root causes of structural unemployment by improving skills matching and labour market flexibility. They can reduce the natural rate of unemployment permanently without creating inflationary pressure, unlike demand-side policies which may cause inflation if the economy is near capacity. Disadvantage: Supply-side policies have long time lags - retraining programmes take years to bear fruit, during which workers remain unemployed. In contrast, demand-side policies (fiscal stimulus, interest rate cuts) can boost employment relatively quickly, which matters during acute recessions when immediate relief is needed.' },
      { q: 'Explain the concept of crowding out and why it might limit the effectiveness of expansionary fiscal policy.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Crowding out occurs when increased government borrowing to fund fiscal expansion raises interest rates in financial markets as the government competes for loanable funds. Higher interest rates discourage private sector investment and consumer borrowing, partially or fully offsetting the intended stimulus effect. Financial crowding out is most significant when: the economy is near full capacity, financial markets are tight, and the multiplier is small due to other leakages. However, in a recession with low interest rates and spare capacity, crowding out is less likely because banks have excess reserves and private sector demand for loans is weak.' },
      { q: 'Using a Keynesian AD/AS diagram, explain how expansionary fiscal policy can increase real GDP without causing inflation when there is spare capacity.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'On a Keynesian AS diagram, the aggregate supply curve is horizontal (perfectly elastic) at low levels of output where there is significant spare capacity, then slopes upward as the economy approaches full capacity, and becomes vertical at full employment output (Yfe). When the economy is in recession with output well below Yfe, an increase in government spending shifts the AD curve to the right from AD to AD1. Because the economy is on the horizontal section of the AS curve, this increases real GDP from Y to Y1 without raising the price level. The output gap closes as unemployed resources are brought back into production. The multiplier amplifies the initial fiscal stimulus as the increase in incomes generates further rounds of spending. However, if AD is pushed beyond the full employment level, inflation results as the economy hits capacity constraints.' },
      { q: 'Draw and explain a diagram showing the multiplier effect of an increase in government spending on national income.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'On a Keynesian cross (45-degree line) diagram, aggregate expenditure (AE) is on the vertical axis and national income (Y) on the horizontal. The initial equilibrium is where the AE line crosses the 45-degree line at Y1. An increase in government spending (delta G) shifts the AE line upward by the amount of the spending increase. The new equilibrium is at Y2, where the new AE line intersects the 45-degree line. The increase in national income (Y2 - Y1) is greater than the initial increase in government spending, demonstrating the multiplier effect. The multiplier equals 1/(1-MPC) or 1/MPW. Each round of spending generates income, of which a fraction (MPC) is re-spent domestically and a fraction leaks out through savings, taxation and imports. The total effect is the sum of this geometric series.' },
      { q: 'Using a Keynesian AD/AS diagram, illustrate why expansionary fiscal policy may cause inflation rather than higher output when the economy is at full capacity.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a Keynesian AS curve that is horizontal at low output, upward-sloping near full capacity, and vertical at the full-employment level Yfe. Assume the economy starts at Yfe where AS is vertical. An increase in government spending shifts AD right from AD to AD1, but because AS is vertical at Yfe, real output cannot rise - resources are already fully employed. The full effect of the AD shift falls on the price level, which rises from P to P1, producing pure demand-pull inflation. This crowding out of real activity by higher prices is why the output gap matters for policy timing - the same fiscal stimulus that boosts GDP during a recession will only raise inflation if delivered during a boom.' },
      { q: 'Explain how the size of automatic stabilisers in an economy depends on the structure of the tax and welfare system.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Automatic stabilisers are tax and benefit changes that occur without discretionary action, dampening the economic cycle. A more progressive income tax with high marginal rates at higher incomes means that during a boom, larger fractions of additional income are taxed away, cooling demand automatically; during a slump, falling incomes mean a more-than-proportional reduction in tax, supporting disposable income. Generous unemployment benefits and means-tested welfare expand automatically when joblessness rises, sustaining consumer spending. Countries with flat tax structures and limited welfare states (e.g. some US states) therefore have weaker stabilisers and more volatile cycles, while Nordic economies with steep progressivity and large welfare states experience milder fluctuations.' },
      { q: 'Using a money market diagram, illustrate how an increase in money supply via quantitative easing affects market interest rates.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a money market diagram with a vertical money supply curve MS, a downward-sloping money demand curve MD (responsive to interest rates), and initial equilibrium at interest rate i. Quantitative easing shifts the money supply curve right from MS to MS1, as the central bank creates new money and uses it to buy government bonds from financial institutions. The new equilibrium is at a lower interest rate i1 < i, because banks now have excess reserves and compete to lend, pushing rates down. Lower rates stimulate borrowing, consumption, and investment, raising aggregate demand. The asset-price channel (higher bond and equity prices producing a wealth effect) reinforces this stimulus and is often the dominant transmission channel.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that monetary policy is more effective than fiscal policy for managing the UK economy.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for monetary policy: Greater flexibility - the MPC can change rates quickly in response to data. Political independence reduces short-term political interference. Can be adjusted monthly versus annual budgets. Avoids crowding out effects of government borrowing. Arguments for fiscal policy: More direct impact on aggregate demand (especially government spending). More effective in a liquidity trap when interest rates are at the lower bound. Can be targeted at specific sectors or regions. Automatic stabilisers provide immediate counter-cyclical support. Limitations of monetary policy: Transmission mechanism is slow and uncertain. Ineffective at zero lower bound. Distributional effects may worsen inequality (QE inflates asset prices). Arguments against fiscal policy: Political/implementation lags. Risk of crowding out. Budget constraints limit scope. Evaluation: Neither is universally superior. The optimal approach depends on economic circumstances: monetary policy for normal demand management, fiscal policy for severe downturns or when monetary policy is exhausted. In practice, coordination between both provides the most effective policy mix.' },
      { q: 'Assess the extent to which austerity was an appropriate response to the UK\'s fiscal position after 2010.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for austerity: The deficit exceeded 10% of GDP, risking credit rating downgrades and rising borrowing costs. Reducing the deficit maintained market confidence and avoided a potential debt crisis (as seen in Greece). Spending cuts signalled fiscal discipline and helped anchor inflation expectations. Long-term, sustainable public finances are necessary for future policy flexibility. Arguments against: Premature austerity during a fragile recovery risked a double-dip recession through the negative multiplier. The deficit was largely cyclical and would have reduced with growth. Cutting spending reduced demand when the private sector was also deleveraging. Infrastructure and human capital cuts reduce future productive capacity. International evidence suggests multipliers are larger during recessions, magnifying the contractionary effect. Evaluation: Timing was arguably premature - the recovery was not yet self-sustaining. A more gradual consolidation profile, combined with infrastructure investment, might have achieved fiscal sustainability with less damage to growth and public services.' }
    ]
  },
  '3.1': {
    name: 'Globalisation, Trade & Global Business',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define comparative advantage.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Comparative advantage exists when a country can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country. Even if one country is more efficient at producing everything (absolute advantage), both countries benefit from specialising in goods where their opportunity cost is lowest and trading.' },
      { q: 'State two arguments in favour of free trade.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two arguments: (1) Specialisation in what each country produces relatively most efficiently increases total world output and allows every country to consume more than it could produce alone. (2) Free trade increases competition, forcing domestic firms to become more efficient and innovative, benefiting consumers through lower prices and greater choice.' },
      { q: 'Define a tariff.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'A tariff is a tax imposed by a government on imported goods. It raises the price of imports above the world price, making domestic production relatively more competitive and reducing the volume of imports while generating tax revenue for the government.' },
      { q: 'State two factors that cause a currency to appreciate under a floating exchange rate system.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two factors: (1) An increase in interest rates relative to other countries attracts hot money inflows as investors seek higher returns, increasing demand for the currency. (2) An increase in demand for the country\'s exports raises demand for the currency on foreign exchange markets as overseas buyers need to purchase it to pay for goods.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain, using a diagram, the welfare effects of imposing a tariff on imported steel.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'A tariff raises the domestic price of steel above the world price (from Pw to Pw + tariff). Effects: (1) Consumers pay more and buy less. (2) Domestic producers gain - they expand output and earn a wider margin at the higher price. (3) Government gains tariff revenue on remaining imports (quantity imported x tariff per unit). (4) Deadweight welfare loss occurs in two triangles: one from inefficient domestic production replacing cheaper imports (productive inefficiency), another from reduced consumption. Net welfare effect is negative as the deadweight losses exceed any benefits, though domestic producers and the government gain at consumers\' expense.' },
      { q: 'Explain why the J-curve effect occurs following a depreciation of the currency.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'The J-curve shows the current account initially worsening after depreciation before eventually improving. In the short run: import prices rise immediately in domestic currency but volumes are slow to adjust because consumers and firms have existing contracts, limited information about alternatives, and switching costs. Export volumes also take time to increase as foreign buyers need time to recognise cheaper prices. Therefore, the higher import bill (same volume at higher prices) worsens the trade balance initially. In the long run: as contracts expire and consumers adjust, import volumes fall and export volumes rise (assuming Marshall-Lerner condition is met). The trade balance then improves past its original level, creating the characteristic J-shape.' },
      { q: 'Explain two ways in which globalisation may negatively affect workers in developed countries.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Effect 1: Offshoring of manufacturing to low-wage economies reduces demand for low-skilled workers in developed countries. Factory closures cause structural unemployment in regions dependent on manufacturing, with workers lacking skills for growing service sectors. Effect 2: Increased labour market competition puts downward pressure on wages for low-skilled workers. The threat of relocation gives employers bargaining power, and immigration of workers willing to accept lower wages can suppress wage growth at the bottom of the income distribution.' },
      { q: 'Explain the difference between trade creation and trade diversion when countries form a customs union.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Trade creation occurs when membership replaces high-cost domestic production with lower-cost imports from a partner country. This improves efficiency and welfare as resources shift to where comparative advantage lies. Example: UK consumers buying cheaper French wine instead of expensive domestic production. Trade diversion occurs when the common external tariff causes imports to switch from a more efficient non-member to a less efficient member country. This reduces welfare as trade flows are distorted away from the lowest-cost producer. Example: the UK buying wine from a higher-cost EU producer instead of a cheaper Chilean one because the external tariff makes Chilean wine more expensive. A customs union increases welfare only if trade creation exceeds trade diversion.' },
      { q: "Using a supply and demand diagram for a domestic market, explain how opening that market to imports affects the domestic price, domestic output and the quantity imported.", marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: "Diagram (2 marks): a domestic supply and demand diagram with the closed-economy equilibrium at P1 Q1, and a horizontal world price line drawn below P1. Mark the quantity domestic firms supply at the world price (Qs), the quantity domestic consumers demand at the world price (Qd), and label the gap Qd - Qs as imports. Explanation (2 marks): with the market open, domestic buyers can buy at the world price, so the domestic price falls from P1 to the world price. Domestic producers move down their supply curve and supply less, because some of them cannot cover their costs at the lower price; domestic consumers move down the demand curve and buy more. The gap between the two is filled by imports. Consumers pay less and buy more, while the least efficient domestic producers contract or exit - which is why trade liberalisation produces concentrated losses among domestic producers and thinly spread gains among consumers." },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, illustrate the welfare effects of imposing an import quota on steel.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a domestic supply and demand diagram with a horizontal world supply curve at the world price Pw. Under free trade, domestic firms supply Qs and consumers buy Qd, with imports filling the gap. An import quota that limits imports to a fixed quantity Qq raises the domestic price to Pq, at which point domestic supply plus the quota equals domestic demand. Consumers are worse off (higher price, lower consumption). Domestic producers gain (more output at a higher price). Quota holders or foreign exporters capture the rent equal to (Pq - Pw) x Qq - unlike a tariff, the government collects no revenue from this rent. Two deadweight welfare losses arise: from inefficient domestic production replacing cheaper imports, and from consumers priced out of the market.' },
      { q: 'Explain two reasons why a country may impose capital controls on inflows of foreign portfolio investment.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Reason 1: financial stability. Large hot money inflows can fuel asset bubbles in housing or equities, and when sentiment reverses the sudden outflow causes sharp depreciation, banking stress, and recession - the pattern seen in the 1997 Asian financial crisis. Capital controls slow inflows and reduce the risk of disorderly sudden stops, giving the central bank space to respond. Reason 2: managing the exchange rate. Heavy portfolio inflows appreciate the currency, hurting exporters and worsening the current account - a Dutch-disease-style problem. Targeted controls such as Brazil\'s IOF tax on portfolio inflows limit this appreciation and preserve export competitiveness, while allowing more stable FDI to continue.' },
      { q: 'Using a foreign exchange market diagram, explain how higher domestic interest rates lead to an appreciation of the currency.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a foreign exchange market with the exchange rate (e.g. £/$) on the y-axis and quantity of pounds traded on the x-axis. Demand for pounds slopes downward; supply slopes upward. Higher UK interest rates attract hot money inflows as international investors require pounds to buy UK bonds and bank deposits offering better returns. Demand for pounds shifts right from D to D1; the supply curve may also shift left as UK investors keep funds at home rather than moving abroad. The new equilibrium exchange rate is higher - the pound appreciates against the dollar. This makes UK exports more expensive abroad and imports cheaper, dampening net exports but reducing imported inflation; it is one of the main transmission channels of monetary policy.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain the effect of a per-unit subsidy to a domestic industry on the level of imports and domestic production.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a domestic supply (S) and demand (D) diagram, with the world price line Pw horizontal. Without intervention, domestic producers supply Qs0 at Pw, consumers buy Qd, and imports fill the gap (Qd - Qs0). A per-unit subsidy to domestic producers shifts their supply curve down/right from S to S+sub, so at the world price Pw they now supply a larger quantity Qs1. Domestic demand at the unchanged world price is still Qd, so imports fall by (Qs1 - Qs0). The government bears the fiscal cost (subsidy x Qs1). Compared to a tariff, consumers do not pay a higher price - the welfare loss falls on taxpayers instead - which is why subsidies are politically easier even though they distort production decisions.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that protectionism is never justified in a modern global economy.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments against protectionism: Restricts consumer choice and raises prices. Invites retaliation, potentially triggering trade wars that reduce global welfare. Protects inefficient industries, misallocating resources. Creates rent-seeking behaviour and lobbying distortions. Reduces competitive pressure for innovation. WTO rules restrict protectionism for good reason. Arguments for selective protectionism: Infant industry argument - temporary protection allows new industries to achieve economies of scale before competing globally (East Asian success stories). National security - strategic industries (defence, food, energy) should not be dependent on imports. Anti-dumping - protection against predatory pricing by foreign firms. Environmental/labour standards - preventing a race to the bottom by restricting imports produced under exploitative conditions. Evaluation: Blanket protectionism is harmful, but selective, temporary, and well-designed interventions may be justified in specific circumstances. The key questions are: who benefits, who bears the cost, is it temporary, and does it address a genuine market failure? The risk of government failure in "picking winners" means protectionist arguments should be treated sceptically.' },
      { q: 'Assess the extent to which a depreciating exchange rate can solve a country\'s current account deficit.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'How depreciation helps: Makes exports cheaper in foreign currency, boosting demand. Makes imports more expensive in domestic currency, reducing demand. If Marshall-Lerner condition is met (sum of export and import PED > 1), the trade balance improves. Limitations: J-curve effect means deterioration before improvement - firms need time to adjust. If demand for exports and imports is inelastic, the volume change is insufficient to offset price effects. Depreciation raises import costs, potentially fuelling cost-push inflation that erodes competitiveness. Competitive devaluation invites retaliation from trading partners. Fundamental structural problems (low productivity, weak manufacturing base) are not solved by exchange rate changes alone. Exchange rate may not depreciate sufficiently in a managed float. Evaluation: Depreciation is a necessary but insufficient condition for correcting structural deficits. It provides breathing space but must be accompanied by supply-side reforms to improve underlying competitiveness. The UK\'s persistent deficit despite significant sterling falls suggests structural rather than purely exchange-rate-driven problems.' }
    ]
  },
  '3.2': {
    name: 'Global Labour Markets, Inequality & Redistribution',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define absolute poverty.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Absolute poverty exists where a household\'s income is too low to meet the basic needs required for survival, such as food, clean water, shelter, clothing and basic healthcare. It is judged against a fixed standard rather than against the incomes of others - the World Bank international extreme poverty line, revised to $3.00 a day in 2025 prices, is the most widely used benchmark.' },
      { q: 'Define the Gini coefficient.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'The Gini coefficient is a statistical measure of income or wealth inequality within a country, ranging from 0 (perfect equality, where every household has the same income) to 1 (perfect inequality, where one household has all the income). It is derived from the Lorenz curve. UK disposable income inequality was around 0.33 in the financial year ending 2024 (ONS).' },
      { q: 'Define economic migration.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Economic migration is the movement of people from one country to another primarily in order to work or to obtain a higher income. It is driven by push factors in the source country, such as unemployment, low wages or instability, and pull factors in the destination country, such as higher pay, better job prospects and stronger public services.' },
      { q: 'State three ways in which a government can redistribute income.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Three ways: (1) Progressive taxation, where the proportion of income paid in tax rises with income, so higher earners contribute a larger share. (2) Transfer payments such as Universal Credit, the State Pension and disability benefits, which raise the incomes of lower-income households without requiring output in return. (3) Provision of public services free at the point of use, such as the NHS and state education, which are benefits in kind worth a much larger share of income to poorer households.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain two effects of large-scale immigration of working-age labour on a host economy.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Effect 1: an increase in the supply of labour. The labour supply curve shifts right, easing skills shortages, allowing firms to fill vacancies and raising the economy\'s productive capacity, which shifts long-run aggregate supply to the right. Where migrants concentrate in low-skilled occupations, the wage in those occupations may face downward pressure. Effect 2: an increase in aggregate demand and the public finances. Migrant workers consume goods and services and pay income tax and National Insurance; because economic migrants are typically of working age, they tend to be net fiscal contributors in the short run. Against this, larger populations add to demand for housing, school places and healthcare, so the net effect depends on the skills profile of migrants and on whether public investment keeps pace. UK net migration was 171,000 in the year to December 2025, down sharply from a peak of around 900,000 in the year to June 2023.' },
      { q: 'Explain the difference between outsourcing and offshoring.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Outsourcing means contracting an external business to carry out an activity the firm previously performed in-house, such as a retailer handing its payroll or IT support to a specialist supplier. Ownership of the activity passes outside the firm, but it may remain in the same country. Offshoring means relocating an activity to a different country, such as a UK manufacturer moving assembly to Poland. The activity may still be owned by the firm. The two overlap when a firm offshores and outsources at the same time - for example a UK bank contracting an Indian company to run its call centres - and both are driven by lower unit labour costs, access to skills and the falling cost of communication.' },
      { q: 'Explain the difference between absolute and relative poverty and why both measures matter.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Absolute poverty is defined by a fixed standard below which basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water cannot be met - typically measured by the World Bank extreme poverty line of $3.00 a day, which replaced the $2.15 line in 2025. Relative poverty is defined in relation to the median income of a particular society, for example a household income below 60% of the UK median. Both matter because absolute poverty captures life-threatening deprivation and is most relevant to low-income countries, while relative poverty captures social exclusion and the inability to take part in normal social life, which matters most in richer countries where basic survival is not the issue. A country can eliminate absolute poverty while relative poverty rises, if growth is concentrated at the top of the distribution, so effective policy has to address both.' },
      { q: 'Using a Lorenz curve diagram, explain how the Gini coefficient measures income inequality and how a progressive tax system would alter the curve.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'The Lorenz curve plots the cumulative percentage of income (vertical axis) against the cumulative percentage of the population ranked from poorest to richest (horizontal axis). The 45-degree line represents perfect equality. The further the Lorenz curve bows below this line, the greater the inequality. The Gini coefficient is the ratio of the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve (area A) to the total area below the line of equality (A + B). A Gini of 0 means perfect equality and 1 means perfect inequality. A progressive tax system takes a larger proportion of income from higher earners, reducing their post-tax income relative to lower earners. This shifts the Lorenz curve closer to the 45-degree line, shrinking area A and so reducing the Gini coefficient for disposable income.' },
      { q: 'Explain two effects on the source economy when large numbers of skilled workers emigrate.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Effect 1: a loss of human capital, often called brain drain. When doctors, engineers and teachers leave, the source country loses the return on the education it financed, key public services are left understaffed and productivity growth slows. This is particularly damaging where the state paid for training and the shortage is concentrated in health or education. Effect 2: an inflow of remittances. Migrants send money home to their families, which raises household incomes, funds schooling and small business investment, and provides a relatively stable source of foreign currency that supports the current account. For several low and middle income countries remittances exceed both foreign direct investment and official aid. The net effect depends on the scale of the skills lost relative to the value of remittances and on whether migrants eventually return with additional skills and capital.' },
      { q: 'Using a labour market diagram, explain how an increase in the supply of migrant labour affects the equilibrium wage and employment in a low-skilled occupation.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a labour market with the wage rate on the vertical axis and quantity of labour on the horizontal axis. The demand for labour (the marginal revenue product curve) slopes downward and the supply of labour slopes upward, with equilibrium at wage W and employment L. An inflow of migrant workers willing to take low-skilled work shifts the labour supply curve to the right from S to S1. The new equilibrium is at a lower wage W1 and a higher level of employment L1: firms take on more workers because labour has become cheaper. Existing workers in that occupation face lower pay, while employers and consumers gain from lower costs. Two qualifications matter: if migrants also raise demand for goods and services, the MRP curve shifts right too, offsetting part of the wage fall; and a binding wage floor such as the National Living Wage prevents the wage falling below £12.71 an hour, so the adjustment shows up as a queue of applicants rather than as lower pay.' },
      { q: 'Explain how the UK National Living Wage redistributes income, and give two limitations of using it to reduce poverty.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'The National Living Wage is a statutory wage floor, set at £12.71 an hour for workers aged 21 and over from April 2026. By forcing employers to pay above the market clearing wage for low-skilled labour, it transfers income from firms\' profits (and from consumers, where higher costs are passed on in prices) to low-paid workers, compressing the wage distribution and lowering the Gini coefficient for earnings. Limitation 1: it only reaches households that contain someone in work. Many of the poorest households are workless - pensioners, the long-term sick, the unemployed - so a higher wage floor does nothing for them. Limitation 2: gains can be clawed back. Higher gross pay pushes workers into income tax and National Insurance and withdraws means-tested benefits such as Universal Credit, so the increase in disposable income is far smaller than the increase in the wage - the poverty trap. A third risk is that where labour demand is elastic, employers respond by cutting hours or jobs rather than absorbing the cost.' },
      { q: 'Using a labour market diagram, explain why a minimum wage can raise both pay and employment when the employer has monopsony power.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a labour market with an upward-sloping labour supply curve (the average cost of labour), a marginal cost of labour curve lying above it, and a downward-sloping marginal revenue product curve. A monopsonist hires where the marginal cost of labour equals the marginal revenue product, at employment Lm, and pays the wage Wm read off the supply curve - below both the competitive wage and the workers\' marginal revenue product. Now impose a minimum wage at Wmin, set between Wm and the competitive wage. Up to the point where the wage floor cuts the supply curve, the employer can no longer drive the wage down by hiring fewer workers, so the marginal cost of labour becomes horizontal at Wmin. The firm now hires where Wmin equals the marginal revenue product, at employment Lmin, which is above Lm. Both pay and employment therefore rise. This is the main reason the Low Pay Commission has been able to raise the National Living Wage substantially without the large job losses that a competitive model would predict, although a floor set far above the competitive wage would still reduce employment.' },
      { q: 'Explain two reasons why international aid may fail to improve welfare in a recipient country.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Reason 1: weak institutions and corruption. Where the rule of law is poor and public financial management is weak, aid can be diverted to elites, spent on prestige projects or used to shore up political support, so it never reaches the intended beneficiaries. Aid can also make governments accountable to donors rather than to their own citizens, weakening the incentive to build a domestic tax base. Reason 2: the design of the aid itself. Tied aid requires the recipient to buy goods and services from the donor country, which may be more expensive or less appropriate than local alternatives, and unpredictable multi-year flows make it impossible to plan long-term health or education programmes. Aid in kind can also depress local prices - food aid undercutting domestic farmers is the classic example. These problems are made worse by falling volumes: the UK cut official development assistance from the 0.7% of gross national income target to 0.5%, and it falls again to 0.3% of GNI - around £9 billion - from 2027.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that international migration brings net economic benefits to a host economy such as the UK.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments that it brings net benefits: migration raises the supply of labour, easing skills shortages in health, social care, construction and agriculture and allowing firms to expand rather than turn away orders. Because economic migrants are overwhelmingly of working age, they tend to be net fiscal contributors, paying income tax and National Insurance while making relatively little use of pensions and long-term care. Migrants also raise aggregate demand, and a larger, more flexible labour force shifts long-run aggregate supply to the right, raising potential output and easing wage-driven inflationary pressure. Skilled migration brings in human capital the UK did not pay to train. Arguments against: the gains are unevenly distributed. Low-skilled workers already in the country may see downward pressure on wages and worse bargaining power in occupations where migrant labour is concentrated. Population growth adds to demand for housing, school places and healthcare, and if public investment does not keep pace the pressure shows up as congestion and queues. Easy access to cheap labour may also weaken the incentive for firms to invest in automation, holding back productivity growth. Fiscal effects depend heavily on composition: dependants and low earners are more likely to be net recipients. Evaluation: the evidence suggests aggregate GDP rises clearly, while GDP per head effects are small and depend on the skills profile of arrivals; the distributional and infrastructure effects are the real policy problem. Net migration has fallen sharply, from a peak of around 900,000 in the year to June 2023 to 171,000 in the year to December 2025, which shifts the debate from managing volume towards ensuring that the migration that does occur is matched to genuine skills shortages and backed by investment in housing and public services.' },
      { q: 'Assess the extent to which reducing inequality should be a key goal of government policy.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for prioritising inequality reduction: high inequality limits human capital development, because poorer households cannot invest in education, training and health, which wastes talent and lowers social mobility. Concentrated wealth can lead to regulatory capture, with institutions serving those at the top. Research by the OECD and IMF suggests very high inequality can slow growth by limiting domestic demand and causing under-investment in the skills of the bottom of the distribution. The capability approach argues that policy should expand the real freedoms of all citizens, not merely raise the average. Arguments against: some inequality reflects differences in effort, skill and risk-taking and provides the incentive to work, save and innovate. Aggressive redistribution through very high marginal tax rates can weaken those incentives, encourage avoidance and emigration, and create poverty traps where extra earnings are largely withdrawn through tax and lost benefits - the equity-efficiency trade-off. Measurement is also contested: the Gini coefficient summarises the whole distribution in one number and hides where inequality actually sits, and it ignores wealth, which in the UK is distributed far more unequally than income. Evaluation: extreme inequality is damaging and worth acting against, but the target matters more than the headline number. Policy that improves equality of opportunity - early years education, technical training, access to healthcare and anti-discrimination law - is likely to raise both equity and efficiency, whereas rigid equality of outcome risks the incentive costs. With UK disposable income inequality broadly flat at a Gini of around 0.33, the stronger case is for targeted action on wealth inequality and on the poverty trap rather than for a general levelling of income.' }
    ]
  },
  '4.1': {
    name: 'Competition & Market Power',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define allocative efficiency.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Allocative efficiency occurs when resources are distributed in a way that maximises consumer welfare. It is achieved when price equals marginal cost (P = MC), meaning the value consumers place on the last unit equals the cost of producing it. No reallocation could make anyone better off without making someone else worse off.' },
      { q: 'State two barriers to entry that might exist in a monopoly market.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two barriers: (1) Legal barriers such as patents that grant exclusive rights to produce a product for a set period, preventing imitation. (2) High sunk costs and capital requirements that deter potential entrants because the investment cannot be recovered if entry fails.' },
      { q: 'Define a contestable market.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'A contestable market is one where barriers to entry and exit are low or non-existent, meaning that the threat of potential competition disciplines existing firms to behave as if in a competitive market, even if there are few actual competitors. Hit-and-run entry is possible.' },
      { q: 'State two conditions necessary for price discrimination to be possible.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Two conditions: (1) The firm must have some degree of market power (be a price maker, not a price taker) to set different prices. (2) The firm must be able to separate consumers into groups with different price elasticities of demand and prevent resale (arbitrage) between the groups.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain why firms in an oligopoly may engage in non-price competition rather than price competition.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Oligopolistic firms are interdependent - each considers rivals\' reactions. Price cuts trigger retaliation (price wars) that reduce profits for all firms, as illustrated by the kinked demand curve model where demand is elastic for price rises but inelastic for cuts. Non-price competition (advertising, branding, loyalty schemes, product innovation, quality improvements) is preferred because: rivals cannot instantly replicate it, it builds brand loyalty creating less elastic demand, it differentiates products making direct price comparison harder, and it can increase market share without destroying industry profit margins. The prisoners\' dilemma reinforces this - collective interest favours maintaining prices while competing on other dimensions.' },
      { q: 'Explain why a monopoly might be considered more dynamically efficient than a perfectly competitive market.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Dynamic efficiency refers to innovation and technological progress over time. A monopolist may be more dynamically efficient because: (1) Supernormal profits provide internal funds for expensive R&D that competitive firms with only normal profit cannot afford. (2) Patent protection creates temporary monopoly power that rewards innovation - without it, firms would not invest in research they cannot profit from. (3) Schumpeter argued that the prospect of monopoly profits incentivises entrepreneurial risk-taking that drives creative destruction. However, this depends on the monopolist facing some competitive pressure (potential entry, contestability) to avoid complacency.' },
      { q: 'Explain how the theory of contestable markets challenges the traditional structure-conduct-performance model.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'The traditional model suggests market structure (number of firms, concentration) determines conduct (pricing behaviour) and performance (efficiency, profits). Contestability theory (Baumol) challenges this by arguing that the threat of potential entry matters more than actual market structure. Even a monopolist will price competitively if barriers to entry and exit are low, because supernormal profits would attract hit-and-run entrants. This means: a concentrated market can still produce competitive outcomes if contestable; policy should focus on reducing barriers rather than breaking up large firms; sunk costs are the key barrier (not firm numbers); and a market\'s conduct and performance cannot be predicted from structure alone.' },
      { q: 'Explain the economic effects of third-degree price discrimination on consumer welfare.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Third-degree price discrimination charges different prices to different consumer groups (e.g., peak/off-peak rail, student discounts). Effects on welfare: Groups with inelastic demand pay more than under uniform pricing, so they are worse off. Groups with elastic demand pay less - their surplus increases and some may now afford the good who previously could not. Total output may increase compared to single pricing, as some consumers are served who would otherwise be priced out. The firm captures more surplus overall, redistributing it from consumers with inelastic demand to producers. Net welfare effect is ambiguous - it depends on whether the output increase outweighs the redistribution effect.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain why a monopolist produces at a lower output and charges a higher price compared to a perfectly competitive market.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'On a diagram showing a downward-sloping demand (AR) curve, the MR curve lies below it (because the monopolist must cut price on all units to sell more). The monopolist produces where MC = MR at output Qm, then charges the price Pm read from the demand curve at that quantity. In perfect competition, the industry supply curve (sum of MC curves) intersects demand at Qc and Pc, where P = MC (allocative efficiency). Comparing the two: the monopolist produces less (Qm < Qc) and charges more (Pm > Pc). The deadweight welfare loss is shown by the triangle between the demand curve and MC curve from Qm to Qc - these are units where consumers value the good more than it costs to produce, but the monopolist does not produce them because MR < MC. Consumers are worse off under monopoly, as value that would have gone to them is transferred to the producer as supernormal profit.' },
      { q: 'Draw and explain the kinked demand curve model and how it explains price rigidity in oligopoly markets.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'The kinked demand curve model assumes that in an oligopoly, if one firm raises its price, rivals will not follow (hoping to gain market share), making demand elastic above the current price. If the firm cuts its price, rivals will match the cut to protect their market share, making demand inelastic below the current price. This creates a kink in the demand curve at the prevailing price P*. The MR curve has a vertical discontinuity (gap) at the kink. The MC curve can shift within this gap without changing the profit-maximising output or price, explaining why oligopoly prices tend to be stable even when costs change. Limitations: the model does not explain how the initial price was set, assumes asymmetric responses that may not always hold, and empirical evidence for the kink is mixed.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, illustrate how a firm in monopolistic competition can earn supernormal profit in the short run but only normal profit in the long run.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw the short-run diagram: a downward-sloping AR curve (demand) reflects product differentiation; MR lies below AR; the U-shaped ATC sits beneath AR at the profit-maximising output where MR = MC. The shaded rectangle (P - ATC) x Q is short-run supernormal profit. Because barriers to entry are low (product differentiation alone does not block rivals), new firms enter the market with similar products. Each existing firm\'s AR curve shifts left as its market share is eroded, until AR is tangent to ATC at the new profit-maximising output. In long-run equilibrium AR = ATC and the firm earns only normal profit. Importantly, P > MC in both diagrams, so monopolistic competition is allocatively inefficient compared to perfect competition.' },
      { q: 'Using a diagram, explain how third-degree price discrimination by a train operator between peak and off-peak commuters affects total revenue.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw two separate demand curves: peak commuters have inelastic demand (steep curve, must travel to work), off-peak leisure travellers have elastic demand (flat curve, willing to switch days or modes). The firm sets MR = MC in each segment independently, charging a higher price in the peak market and a lower price in the off-peak market. Compared to a single uniform price, this extracts more of what peak travellers are willing to pay and brings in off-peak travellers who would otherwise not buy at the uniform price, raising total revenue and profit. The firm must be able to separate markets (time-of-day restrictions on cheap tickets) and prevent resale (named tickets, ID checks) for the strategy to work. Welfare effects are ambiguous: peak consumers are worse off, off-peak consumers and the firm are better off.' },
      { q: 'Using a labour market diagram, explain how a monopsony employer is able to pay wages below the competitive equilibrium.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw a labour market with an upward-sloping labour supply curve (also the average cost of labour, ACL) and a downward-sloping marginal revenue product curve (MRPL, the demand for labour). For a monopsony, the marginal cost of labour curve (MCL) lies above the supply curve because hiring an additional worker requires raising the wage for all existing workers. The monopsonist maximises profit by hiring where MCL = MRPL at employment level Lm, then reads the wage Wm off the supply curve at Lm. Wm is below both the competitive equilibrium wage W* (where ACL = MRPL) and the marginal revenue product of labour at Lm. The gap between MRPL and Wm represents monopsony exploitation - workers are paid less than the value they add - and employment Lm is also lower than the competitive level L*.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that competition policy should always prevent monopolies from forming.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments for preventing monopolies: Monopolies restrict output below the competitive level, causing deadweight welfare loss. They charge higher prices, transferring surplus from consumers to producers. Lack of competitive pressure leads to X-inefficiency. They may use market power to prevent innovation by potential rivals. Arguments against automatic prevention: Some monopolies are natural monopolies where single-firm production minimises costs (utilities, railways). Breaking them up would raise average costs. Monopoly profits fund innovation (Schumpeter) and dynamic efficiency may outweigh static inefficiency. Temporary monopoly (patents) incentivises investment in R&D. Contestable monopolies may already behave competitively. The process of monopolisation through superior efficiency should not be punished. Competition policy should distinguish between monopolies that arise from efficiency and those that exploit market power. Evaluation: A blanket policy against monopolies would be harmful. The appropriate approach is case-by-case assessment weighing static efficiency losses against dynamic efficiency gains, market contestability, and the potential for regulation to achieve competitive outcomes without structural change.' },
      { q: 'Assess whether oligopoly is more likely to result in collusion or competition between firms.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Factors favouring collusion: Few firms make monitoring and coordination easier. Homogeneous products reduce incentives to compete on price. High barriers to entry protect collusive profits. Stable demand conditions reduce uncertainty. Repeated interaction creates opportunities for punishment strategies. Factors favouring competition: Collusion is illegal and risks heavy fines. Firms have individual incentives to cheat (prisoners\' dilemma). Different cost structures make agreement on price difficult. Product differentiation creates different optimal prices for each firm. Demand fluctuations make cheating detection harder. Transparency of pricing (online markets) makes tacit collusion both easier to achieve and easier for regulators to detect. Evaluation: The outcome depends on specific market characteristics. Markets with few firms, homogeneous products, and stable demand (e.g., OPEC) tend towards collusion, while markets with many oligopolists, differentiated products, and rapid innovation (e.g., smartphones) tend towards vigorous competition. Effective competition policy and market design can tip the balance towards competitive outcomes.' }
    ]
  },
  '4.2': {
    name: 'Market Failure, Policy Impact & Financial Risk',
    green: [
      { q: 'Define government failure.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Government failure occurs when intervention intended to correct a market failure leads to a net welfare loss, leaving the allocation of resources worse than it would have been without the intervention. It arises from causes such as imperfect information about the size of an externality, unintended consequences, administrative costs, regulatory capture and short-term political incentives.' },
      { q: 'Define systemic risk.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Systemic risk is the risk that the failure of one financial institution, or of one market, spreads through the financial system and threatens its overall stability. It arises because banks are linked by interbank lending, shared exposures and common assets, so losses at one institution trigger losses and withdrawals elsewhere - contagion - rather than staying contained.' },
      { q: 'Distinguish between risk and uncertainty.', marks: 2, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Risk describes a situation where the possible outcomes are known and probabilities can be attached to them, so the exposure can be priced, hedged or insured - for example an insurer pricing the chance of a house fire from historical data. Uncertainty describes a situation where the outcomes or their probabilities cannot be known, such as the effect of a new technology or a future political shock, so it cannot be insured against and is instead managed through diversification and caution.' },
      { q: 'State three functions of the financial sector.', marks: 3, tier: 'green', modelAnswer: 'Three functions: (1) Channelling savings into investment, by taking deposits from households and lending them to firms and governments. (2) Providing forward markets in currencies and commodities, allowing firms to fix a price or an exchange rate for a future transaction and so manage risk. (3) Providing a market for equities, so that companies can raise long-term finance by issuing shares and savers can buy and sell them. The sector also facilitates the exchange of goods and services through the payments system and provides insurance.' }
    ],
    amber: [
      { q: 'Explain why public goods are under-provided across the economy as a whole.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Public goods are non-rival, so one person consuming the good does not reduce the amount available to anyone else, and non-excludable, so those who do not pay cannot be prevented from consuming it. Non-excludability creates the free-rider problem: a rational individual will understate their willingness to pay, expecting to benefit from what others buy. Because the revealed demand curve therefore lies far below the true social demand curve, a profit-seeking firm cannot cover its costs and provides little or none of the good. The result is a complete missing market for goods such as national defence, flood defences, street lighting and the policing of contracts - all of which yield large social benefits across the whole economy. Governments respond by providing them directly and funding them through compulsory taxation, which removes the option of free riding.' },
      { q: 'Explain two ways in which asymmetric information causes market failure in an economy.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Way 1: adverse selection, which occurs before a transaction. In private health or annuity insurance, buyers know more about their own risk than the insurer does. Those most likely to claim are most likely to buy, so claims exceed expectations, premiums rise, low-risk buyers withdraw and the market can unravel - the same logic as the market for used cars, where buyers cannot tell a good car from a poor one so good cars are withdrawn. Way 2: moral hazard, which occurs after a transaction. Once insured or protected, a party has less incentive to take care: a bank that expects to be rescued lends more riskily, and an insured driver may drive less carefully. Both mean price no longer reflects true cost or benefit, so resources are misallocated. Policy responses include compulsory disclosure, licensing and quality standards, excesses and co-payments in insurance, and warranties and independent inspections in second-hand markets.' },
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by factor immobility and why it matters for an economy.', marks: 3, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Factor immobility means that factors of production cannot move freely to where they would be most productively used. Labour faces occupational immobility, where redundant workers lack the skills the expanding industries need, and geographical immobility, where housing costs, family ties and moving costs keep workers in declining regions. Capital can be immobile too, where plant is specialised and its resale value is low. It matters because it prevents the price mechanism from reallocating resources after a shock: unemployment persists alongside unfilled vacancies, actual output stays below potential output, and regional inequality widens. It is one of the main causes of structural unemployment and a central justification for supply-side policies such as retraining, apprenticeships and housing reform.' },
      { q: 'Using an AD/AS diagram, explain how expansionary fiscal policy affects firms and individuals when the economy is already close to full capacity output.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw an AD/AS diagram with a short-run aggregate supply curve that becomes steep and then vertical as output approaches the full capacity level Yfc, and an initial equilibrium close to Yfc. An increase in government spending or a cut in taxation shifts aggregate demand right from AD to AD1, with the multiplier amplifying the initial injection. Because the economy is on the steep section of AS, most of the shift shows up as a rise in the price level from P to P1 and only a small rise in real output. For firms, this means higher selling prices but also rising input and wage costs as spare capacity disappears, and a likely monetary policy response - a higher Bank Rate raises borrowing costs and can appreciate sterling, hurting exporters. For individuals, nominal incomes rise but real incomes may not, as inflation erodes them, and mortgage holders face higher repayments. The same stimulus delivered during a recession, when the economy sits on the flat section of AS, would raise real output with little effect on prices, which is why the output gap determines whether the policy helps or simply adds to inflation.' },
      { q: 'Explain how a rise in Bank Rate is likely to affect firms and individuals differently.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'For firms, a higher Bank Rate raises the cost of bank borrowing and the hurdle rate that an investment project must clear, so marginal projects are postponed and stock levels are cut. Highly geared firms and those with variable-rate debt are hit hardest, while firms holding large cash balances earn more interest. Higher rates also tend to attract capital inflows and appreciate sterling, which squeezes exporters but cheapens imported inputs. For individuals, the effect depends on the balance sheet. Households with variable-rate or maturing fixed-rate mortgages face higher repayments and cut discretionary spending, whereas net savers, particularly older households, gain interest income. Higher rates also reduce house and share prices, creating a negative wealth effect. The Bank of England held Bank Rate at 3.75% in September 2026 on a 6-3 vote, with inflation at 3.1% in August, illustrating the judgement involved: the committee has to weigh the squeeze on borrowers against the need to bring inflation back to the 2% target.' },
      { q: 'Explain two ways in which a UK firm might respond to a sustained depreciation of sterling.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Response 1: change pricing and output. A UK exporter finds its goods cheaper in foreign currency, so it can either hold the foreign price and widen its sterling margin, or cut the foreign price to win market share and expand output. Which it chooses depends on the price elasticity of demand in the export market and on whether it has spare capacity. Response 2: manage the cost side. A firm that imports components now faces higher sterling input costs, squeezing margins. It may hedge by buying currency forward to fix the exchange rate on future purchases, renegotiate supplier contracts, switch to domestic suppliers, or reshore part of the supply chain. In the short run many firms can do neither quickly, because contracts are already fixed and suppliers cannot be changed overnight - one reason the trade balance follows the J-curve, worsening before it improves.' },
      { q: 'Using an AD/AS diagram, explain how a supply-side policy that raises productivity benefits both firms and individuals.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', diagram: true, modelAnswer: 'Draw an AD/AS diagram with a vertical long-run aggregate supply curve at potential output Y, an upward-sloping SRAS and an AD curve. A supply-side policy such as investment in technical training, infrastructure or research and development raises the productivity of labour and capital, shifting LRAS right from LRAS to LRAS1 and SRAS right as unit costs fall. The new equilibrium has higher real output Y1 and a lower price level P1. Firms benefit because unit costs fall, margins widen and the capacity constraint is relaxed, so they can meet rising demand without bidding up wages. Individuals benefit because higher productivity supports higher real wages, employment rises, and lower inflation protects the purchasing power of incomes. The policy also eases the growth-inflation conflict, since output can expand without the demand-pull pressure that a purely demand-side stimulus would create. The drawbacks are long time lags, significant upfront fiscal cost and uncertainty about which interventions actually raise productivity.' },
      { q: 'Explain two ways in which the financial sector supports firms, and one risk that arises from them.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Way 1: it channels household savings into business investment. Banks take deposits and lend to firms, and equity markets allow a company to raise long-term finance by issuing shares without taking on debt, which funds capital spending that the firm could not finance from retained profit alone. Way 2: it allows firms to manage risk. Forward and futures markets let an exporter fix an exchange rate, or an airline fix a fuel price, for a future date, so a volatile input price does not threaten the business; insurance markets pool other risks. The risk that arises is that the same instruments can be used to speculate rather than to hedge. Leveraged positions taken in the expectation of price movements can inflate asset bubbles, and because institutions are linked by interbank lending and shared exposures, losses at one can spread across the system as systemic risk - the pattern seen in 2008.' },
      { q: 'Explain how moral hazard and speculation contributed to the 2008 financial crisis and how regulation has since sought to address them.', marks: 4, tier: 'amber', modelAnswer: 'Moral hazard: banks judged too big to fail expected public rescue if their bets went wrong, so they operated with very thin capital buffers and took on risky mortgage lending, keeping the gains while the downside fell on taxpayers. Originating lenders who sold loans on through securitisation had little incentive to check whether borrowers could repay. Speculation: rapidly rising house prices encouraged borrowing and buying in the expectation of further gains rather than on the basis of rental yields or incomes, inflating a bubble; when prices turned, mortgage-backed securities were repriced, interbank lending froze and losses spread across the system. The regulatory response has been to raise minimum capital and liquidity requirements under Basel III, to ring-fence retail banking from investment banking in the UK from 2019, to give the Bank of England\'s Financial Policy Committee a mandate for systemic risk, to split conduct regulation (the Financial Conduct Authority) from prudential supervision (the Prudential Regulation Authority), and to create resolution and bail-in powers so that creditors rather than taxpayers absorb losses.' }
    ],
    red: [
      { q: 'Evaluate the view that government intervention to correct market failure across the economy does more harm than good.', marks: 8, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Arguments that intervention does more harm than good: governments rarely know the true size of an externality, so a tax or subsidy is set too high or too low and creates a fresh distortion. Interventions have unintended consequences - a minimum alcohol price shifts demand to cross-border purchases, a ban on plastic bags raises demand for heavier alternatives. Regulatory capture means the regulator comes to act in the interests of the industry rather than consumers, and political incentives favour visible short-term measures over efficient ones. Administrative and compliance costs are real resource costs, and intervention can crowd out private provision and blunt incentives. Arguments that intervention does more good than harm: the counterfactual is not an efficient market but a failing one. Pure public goods such as defence and flood protection would simply not be supplied, because free riding means no firm can charge for them. Economy-wide externalities such as greenhouse gas emissions are too large and too diffuse for private bargaining to resolve, as the conditions required by the Coase theorem - clearly defined property rights and negligible transaction costs - do not hold. Asymmetric information in insurance, pensions and financial products causes markets to unravel without disclosure rules and licensing. Factor immobility leaves unemployment alongside unfilled vacancies unless government funds retraining. Evaluation: the question is not whether to intervene but how. The case for intervention is strongest where the market failure is large and well evidenced, the instrument works through the price mechanism rather than against it, and the policy can be reviewed and withdrawn - carbon pricing and tradeable permits are better examples than detailed quantity controls. The correct test is whether the expected welfare gain exceeds the expected cost of intervention including the risk of government failure, not whether intervention is perfect.' },
      { q: 'Assess the effectiveness of financial regulation in reducing systemic risk in the UK.', marks: 6, tier: 'red', modelAnswer: 'Evidence that regulation has worked: banks now hold far more and better quality capital than in 2007 under the Basel III framework, and liquidity requirements mean they can survive a funding shock without an emergency central bank loan. Ring-fencing, in force since 2019, separates retail deposit-taking from investment banking, so losses on trading cannot directly destroy ordinary depositors\' accounts. The Bank of England\'s Financial Policy Committee has an explicit macroprudential mandate and tools such as countercyclical capital buffers and limits on high loan-to-income mortgage lending, and regular stress testing checks resilience against severe scenarios. Resolution and bail-in powers mean creditors, not taxpayers, are first in line to absorb losses, which directly attacks the moral hazard of too big to fail. Limitations: risk has migrated rather than disappeared. A large share of lending now sits with non-bank financial institutions - private credit funds, money market funds, hedge funds - which are far more lightly supervised, and the 2022 gilt market episode showed how leverage outside the banking system can force central bank intervention. Regulation is also backward-looking, designed against the last crisis, while new exposures build in areas such as crypto assets and concentrated cloud and payment providers. Tighter rules raise the cost of credit, which has a real cost in lost investment, and market participants still expect systemically important institutions to be rescued. Evaluation: UK banks are materially more resilient than in 2008 and the direct taxpayer exposure is lower, so regulation has reduced systemic risk without eliminating it. The main remaining weakness is the perimeter of regulation - the more that risky activity is pushed outside the supervised banking system, the less protection the existing framework offers.' }
    ]
  }
};

